# Files that are executed on Linux must keep LF endings no matter which platform they were
# edited on. core.autocrlf is true on the development machine, so without this a shell script
# committed from Windows arrives on the VPS with CRLF and fails with an error that names the
# wrong thing entirely - which is why install.sh is currently run through `sed -i 's/\r$//'`
# before it will execute. That workaround stays as a belt-and-braces measure, but it should
# never be the thing making deployment work.
gpb         text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf

# Go source is LF by convention and gofmt output is compared byte for byte in CI-style checks.
*.go        text eol=lf

# PowerShell stays CRLF: it is Windows-only tooling, and the repo requires these files to carry
# a UTF-8 BOM anyway (PowerShell 5.1 reads a BOM-less file as ANSI).
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
